# Local-only by policy, not by size: the evidence tree is the audit archive whose
# purpose is that a claim can be taken to AWS Support by request id and FULL ARN
# (see check_redaction.py's docstring). It is never distributed; the release path
# is results/ plus the Markdown deliverables, which the redaction gate scans.
evidence/

# The EC2 runner's local bookkeeping: the instance, subnet, security-group and VPC ids it
# provisioned, the code tarball it uploads, and output pulled back from S3 for review. Three of
# those id shapes are redaction targets (check_redaction.py's `vpc-or-subnet-id` pattern), and a
# file that needs a waiver on every run is a file that trains a reader to waive things. The
# runner's SOURCE is tracked; only its resolved ids are not.
runner/.state/

# NOT the sanctioned staging path — `runner/sync.py pull` writes to `runner/.state/incoming/<stamp>/`,
# which the entry above already covers. This entry exists because on 2026-08-13 two pulls were done
# by hand with `aws s3 sync` into `runner/.staging/<stamp>/` instead, to fetch three case files
# quickly; the two stamps then sat there at 55 MB after their contents were merged into `results/`,
# and a scratch copy reached 90,562 KB against an 80,000 KB ceiling. The ceiling caught it, which is
# DEV-P4-36's guard doing its job; the stamps have been moved under `runner/.state/incoming/` and the
# directory is gone. The line stays so that the next hurried `aws s3 sync` costs a wasted 55 MB
# instead of a red gate, and so that a path used as local-only is finally said to be local-only.
runner/.staging/

# Derived build output of the SPA, and NOT skipped by check_redaction.py — the distinction matters.
# These bytes are published (they are what CloudFront serves), so the gate must keep reading them;
# `SKIP_DIRS` deliberately does not list them and `gate_payload.py` scans them again at publish time.
# What they must not be is TRACKED: a committed `dist/` is a second copy of the source that diverges
# the moment anyone edits `site/src` without rebuilding, and a reviewer would have no way to tell
# which one the deployment came from. `npm run build` regenerates them from source in seconds.
# Verified rather than assumed, 2026-08-20: `check_redaction.files()` returns exactly
# `site/dist/index.html` and the two `assets/` files. It does NOT return anything under
# `site/dist/data`, which is a SYMLINK to the payload directory and which `Path.rglob` does not
# descend into — so the payload's own bytes are `gate_payload.py`'s responsibility (113 files there),
# not this gate's. Two gates, two disjoint sets, and neither one covers the other's.
site/dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Environments and caches
.venv/
.venv-*/
__pycache__/
.pytest_cache/
# `ruff check` writes this; it appeared in `tools/repo_diff.py`'s ADDED list the first
# time the lint gate ran here, i.e. it was one push list away from being published.
.ruff_cache/
node_modules/
f1_config/.wheel_cache/
.DS_Store
